{
  "dom_id": "step-e2647",
  "name": "Example: Finding the Horizontal Distance Between Two Points",
  "problem": "What is the distance between the points $(2,4)$  and $(5,4)?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14146131/assets/ce70a8122559f67aac379349fb369e6e7e007e85fa8a13632e7865469804fb6a.png)",
  "worked_solution": "**Method 1** \n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $3$ units, so the distance equals $3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14146131/assets/bd568e789e79574225dbd75e79d4cacb2fbf92beb048f5bdafeb67f74ab029f6.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $y$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14146131/assets/fb2f075f8321aca3823770e28a86a8d9532cb343ba4239f94c2fb9d1cc883b0b.png)\n\nThe point $({\\color{blue}{5}},4)$ is at a distance of $\\color{blue}{5}$ from the $y$-axis, and the point $({\\color{red}{2}},4)$ is at a distance of $\\color{red}{2}$ from the $y$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is ${\\color{blue}{5}} - {\\color{red}{2}} = 3.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
